According to a new report from Intel Market Research, the global HVO Fuel market was valued at USD 3.10 billion in 2025 and is projected to reach USD 15.20 billion by 2034, growing at a robust CAGR of 19.2 % during the forecast period (2026–2034). This expansion reflects a confluence of strong policy momentum, expanding renewable hydrogen capacity, and the inherent technical advantages of Hydrotreated Vegetable Oil (HVO) as a true drop in replacement for conventional diesel.
Hydrotreated Vegetable Oil (HVO) is a renewable diesel produced by hydrogenating waste cooking oils, animal fats or non food vegetable oils, removing oxygen atoms and yielding a paraffinic fuel chemically identical to conventional diesel.
The process delivers a high cetane number, excellent cold weather flow and stability while cutting lifecycle greenhouse gas emissions by up to ninety percent compared with fossil diesel.
Because it can be used as pure HVO100 or blended in any proportion without engine modifications, it functions as a true drop in solution for existing diesel fleets.
