Global capacity expansion continues at pace, with new stand alone biorefineries announced in Texas, the North Sea and Singapore. Long term waste oil contracts and co processing projects lock in low carbon feedstocks, reinforcing supply certainty and supporting the commercial case for HVO Fuel amid tightening decarbonisation mandates.
MARKET CHALLENGES
High Production Cost and Feedstock Volatility
The hydrotreatment pathway demands significant renewable hydrogen and premium quality feedstocks, whose prices fluctuate with agricultural cycles and waste oil collection logistics. Although hydrogen costs have trended lower, the overall cost structure remains above conventional diesel, restricting adoption to customers with dedicated sustainability budgets or incentive driven economics.
Other Challenges
Infrastructure and Logistical Complexity
Maintaining segregated storage, rigorous certification and traceability across the supply chain adds operational overhead. Smaller distributors often lack digital tracking tools, creating bottlenecks that can deter new market entrants.
MARKET RESTRAINTS
Competition from Alternative Decarbonisation Pathways
Battery electric propulsion captures increasing share of light duty segments, while green hydrogen derived e fuels attract investment for heavy duty and maritime applications. Policy incentives that favour zero emission solutions over low carbon liquids erode the relative advantage of HVO Fuel in regions where electrification or hydrogen pathways receive stronger subsidies.
MARKET OPPORTUNITIES
Expansion into Aviation and Marine Sectors
Certification of HVO derived Sustainable Aviation Fuel (SAF) for commercial jet fleets has opened a high value revenue stream, while maritime regulators in Europe and North America mandate lower sulphur, low carbon fuels for short sea shipping. The drop in nature of HVO enables seamless blending with Jet A and marine diesel, simplifying certification and accelerating market uptake. Companies that secure waste oil feedstock contracts and invest in marine grade blending infrastructure are positioned to capture a growing share of these emerging segments.
Regional Market Insights
•	Europe: Europe anchors the market with rigorous renewable diesel blending mandates, extensive pipeline and rail networks, and mature carbon credit schemes. The region’s policy certainty drives long term off take contracts, encouraging refiners to convert existing units and investors to fund stand alone biorefineries.
•	North America: A patchwork of federal and state incentives-including the Renewable Fuel Standard, California’s Low Carbon Fuel Standard and the Inflation Reduction Act credits-creates tradable credits that narrow the cost gap for HVO. Integrated supply chains and growing corporate ESG commitments support rapid fleet adoption.
•	Asia Pacific: Japan and South Korea lead with explicit renewable diesel mandates, while other economies rely on emerging carbon credit frameworks. Feedstock constraints have spurred interest in palm oil residues, municipal waste liquefaction and algae platforms, often under technology licensing partnerships with European firms.
•	South America: Abundant agricultural by products provide a fertile feedstock base, but conversion to HVO requires retrofitting existing biodiesel facilities. Policy levers-blending mandates and renewable fuel subsidies-are stimulating pilot projects that leverage ethanol infrastructure for cost effective scale up.
•	Middle East & Africa: Oil rich nations are exploring diversification into renewable fuels. Early pilot projects focus on municipal solid waste and industrial residues, supported by European technology licensors and nascent carbon credit mechanisms that may unlock future marine fuel demand.
